Capturing and decoding real Oracle wire-protocol traffic
==========================================================

Several tickets in the oracle protocol milestone can only be settled
against the bytes a real Oracle server actually sends - guessing at a
FIXME in src/protocols/oracle.cpp is no substitute for watching a live
handshake. oraproxy and oradecode, in this directory, are the tools for
that.

jnettrace.jar, which used to do this job, is gone (see #9098). It was
from 2009 and threw an IndexOutOfBoundsException partway through the
handshake on modern JDKs, so a capture never reached the interesting
part - a tool that fails mid-handshake is worse than no tool, since the
failure looks like a protocol bug rather than a broken jar.

oraproxy
--------

A small TCP proxy: it listens locally, connects to a real Oracle
host:port, relays one client connection through in both directions, and
writes every byte it sees, tagged by direction, to a capture file.

    ./oraproxy LISTENPORT DESTHOST DESTPORT CAPTUREFILE \
                                [OLDBANNER NEWBANNER [DIRECTIONS]]

It handles exactly one connection, then exits - no need to kill it off
afterward the way jnettrace.jar had to be.

With the optional OLDBANNER/NEWBANNER pair it also rewrites every
occurrence of OLDBANNER to NEWBANNER on the way through, in the direction
DIRECTIONS names ("s" for server->client, the default, "c" for
client->server, or "both"). The two strings must be the same length, so
nothing has to be done about TNS or TTC length fields. See
ocibannerrewritetest below for what this is for.

oradecode
---------

Reads a capture file back and decodes it as a stream of TNS packets: the
8-byte TNS header, the packet type (CONNECT, ACCEPT, DATA, ...), and, for
DATA packets, the TTC function code and (for protocol negotiation) a full
field-by-field decode of the TTIPRO response - version, banner, charset,
graph elements, FDO block, capability arrays. It also decodes the auth
key/value pair stream that follows a client's phase-1 logon request
(AUTH_SESSKEY, AUTH_VFR_DATA, and - for a 12c/pbkdf2 verifier - the
AUTH_PBKDF2_* fields), calling out AUTH_VFR_DATA's flags (the verifier
type - 0x1b25 for 11g, 0x4815 for 12c) and AUTH_SESSKEY's length (96 hex
chars for 11g, 64 for 12c) explicitly. It reports how many of a packet's
bytes it accounted for, so a short or wrong decode shows up instead of
being silently swallowed.

A DATA_DESCRIPTOR packet (type 15) - a lob read's answer chunk or a lob
write's data - is walked in either direction. Its body is 64 bytes: a
more/last flag (2 or 3), a byte count, a segment count followed by one
ub2 size per segment (the count is not always 1), and zero padding. The
bytes the count names ride after the packet's declared end, so the decode
dumps that raw run with the packet, and the next packet is framed where it
really starts. A raw run that is cut off at the end of the capture is
reported with how many of its bytes arrived. Before this, the decode lost
the stream after the first such packet and ended with undecoded trailing
bytes. TTC code 0x0e is named TTC_LOB_AND_BFILE_DATA.

A session whose client negotiates a field version below 4 - 9i sqlplus,
an OCI7 program, a 9i OCI8 client - also gets its calls walked field by
field: the legacy TTI_OPEN, TTI_QUERY, TTI_QUERY2 (with each define and
bind descriptor, and what its flag byte asks for), TTI_FETCH and
TTI_CLOSE requests, the summary object, row header and rows that answer
them, and the describe, return parameters and summary that answer a 10g
style call at that field version. The walk names every field with the
bytes it came from, and ends with a count of the bytes it accounted for:
a walk that stops short says how many bytes it left and dumps them, and
one that runs off the end falls back to a hex dump with the reason. The
layouts are a 10.2 server's (the 10538-* real-server samples below). A
12.2 server answers some calls differently, and a dml answer's rowid
block isn't read, so those walks stop short, which is the walk saying
what it doesn't know, not the server being wrong.

    ./oradecode CAPTUREFILE

The packet type, TTC code and TTI code tables, the TTIPRO body layout,
and the auth key/value pair layout are all taken directly from
src/protocols/oracle.cpp in this repository (see the PACKET_*, TTC_* and
TTI_* defines, putTtiResponse(), and putAuthField()/getAuthField()).

oratoolstest.py
---------------

A hand-run offline check of the two tools against samples/ (run it from
this directory; it needs no database and is not part of test.sh or the
Makefile):

    ./oratoolstest.py [PORT]

First it runs oradecode over every .oraproxy and .cap file in samples/,
and fails on a nonzero exit, a traceback or "undecoded trailing bytes".
It also checks that the six samples that used to stall at their first
data descriptor packet decode to the capture's last packet:
10005-dev-oci23-native-piecewiselobread-smallsdu-realserver-oracle122,
10314-dev-pythonoracledb-thin-main-sqlrelay-before and -after, and
10569-dev-ojdbc-lobs-{noprefetch,prefetch0,prefetch4000}-realserver-
oracle122.

Then it replays captures through a live oraproxy with
ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS=1 (default listen port 15231), playing both ends
from one thread, with the chunks in capture order. Four captures must
arrive with no changes: the ojdbc captures
10569-dev-ojdbc-lobs-realserver-oracle122 and
10701-dev-ojdbc-dml-realserver-oracle122, and the OCI native captures
10562-dev-oci23-native-dtbinds-tzv26-realserver-oracle122 and
oracle122-oci-native-login-select.cap.
oracle122-oci-portable-login-select.cap must arrive with exactly 9
entries rewritten (types 0x19 through 0x21), the ones the data type
message in oracle122-oci-portable-universalreps-login-select.cap has.
10048-dev-oci23api7-universalreps-realserver holds the rewritten bytes,
so the script puts its entries back to the two-representation form first
and checks that the proxy rewrites them again - all but 0xb5, which was
universal to begin with. It prints passed and exits 0 on success. Against
the oraproxy and oradecode from before #10697 it fails 17 checks.

Running a capture
------------------

jdbcoracletest drives oracle.java (via ojdbc8.jar) through oraproxy
against the oracle122 real server (the one with a working login) and
decodes the result. jdbcsqlrelaytest does the same against a local
sqlr-listener speaking the oracle protocol. Both need java, javac and
`make` to have built oracle.class first (`make` in this directory).

jdbcauthkvtest drives oracle.java through oraproxy against the local
oracleprotocol11g and oracleprotocol12c sqlrelay instances
(test/sqlrelay.conf.d/oracleprotocol.conf; it reads each instance's
port from that generated conf file at run time) and greps the decoded
captures for the AUTH_VFR_DATA flags and AUTH_SESSKEY
length each verifier type is expected to put on the wire. This is direct
wire-level evidence for the same thing oci8.cpp's Authentication
section proves indirectly (a correct-password login only succeeds if the
matching verifier crypto ran) - here the verifier type and session key
length are read straight off the wire instead of inferred from login
success. The two sqlrelay instances need to be started first (sqlr-start
against oracleprotocol.conf, or ./test.sh oracleprotocol).

ojdbc
-----

ojdbc.java (built into ojdbc.class by `make` here when a jdk is
configured) is the oracleprotocol suite's ojdbc check. It logs in as
testuser, checks getDatabaseProductVersion() and
getDatabaseMajorVersion(), then creates protocoltestojdbc with CLOB and
BLOB rows of 8000+ characters/bytes, 40000 (past ojdbc's default 32768
lob prefetch), empty and short values, all with plain inserts (each CLOB
built by the database, each BLOB a bound byte array). Then it checks plain
dml on protocoltestojdbcdml: the update counts of executeUpdate() for an
insert, an insert-select, a multi-row update and deletes of 0 and 2 rows,
execute() with getUpdateCount(), a prepared insert, executeBatch() for an
insert and an update, and, with autocommit off, a rollback and a commit
(a second session sees the row only after the commit). executeBatch()
expects a count per statement at version 12 and SUCCESS_NO_INFO (-2) per
statement below that, since ojdbc only asks for the counts from field
version 12.1 on. Then it checks failed statements on one connection: 8
of each kind, more than the instance's maxcursors (default 5) - a new
Statement per failure, closed (ORA-00942), one reused Statement
(ORA-00942), PreparedStatements that fail to parse (ORA-00942 and
ORA-00900), a duplicate insert (ORA-00001), a fetch error (select 1/0,
ORA-01476) and a failed executeBatch() (ORA-00001) - each followed by a
good query on the same connection. A real server also keeps a failed
statement's cursor until the client closes it, so it checks that 4 failed
Statements left open still leave room for a query, that a sixth left open
gets ORA-01000, and that closing them recovers. Last it reads the lob rows
back with getString/getBytes
and getClob/getBlob at the default prefetch size and with
oracle.jdbc.defaultLobPrefetchSize set to 0 and -1. It also selects
BFILE columns and reads each one's directory alias and file name with
getDirAlias() and getName(), which run sys.dbms_lob.fileGetName with the
locator bound. test.sh runs it
against the 12.1, 10.2 and 11.2 listeners. To run it by hand:

    java -cp ojdbc8.jar:. ojdbc PORT MAJORVERSION

ociselect and ocibannerrewritetest
-----------------------------------

ociselect (ociselect.cpp, built by `make` here) is a minimal OCI client:
log in, run one select, print the rows. oci8.cpp in this directory is
the full OCI test; ociselect is deliberately as small as an OCI
login-plus-query can be, so a capture of it holds nothing but the login
and the one query.

    ./ociselect CONNECTSTRING [USER PASSWORD [QUERY]]

With OCISELECT_SERVER_VERSION=1 in the environment it also calls
OCIServerVersion right after the login and prints the banner that comes
back, so the capture holds a TTI_VERSION (function code 0x3B) request and
its response. It is off by default, so an ordinary capture is unchanged.

ociloginretry.cpp, built by `make` here as well, is a minimal OCI client
that retries a failed login on one connection: one OCIServerAttach, then a
selectable sequence of OCISessionBegin attempts on that same attach, with
different credentials each time and no detach or reattach in between.

    ./ociloginretry CONNECTSTRING \
      [--attempts=wrongpassword,unknownuser,emptypassword,correctpassword] \
                     [USER PASSWORD]

It is oci8.cpp's Authentication section - the #9311 sequence - call for
call, with nothing else in the program, so a capture of it holds only the
attach, the login attempts and the logoff. oci8.cpp logs in twice, creates
tables and runs a hundred other statements around that section, which makes
a capture of it far too noisy to read the retry's byte layout out of - the
same reason oci7describe.cpp and oci7bind.cpp exist next to oci7.cpp.
CONNECTSTRING is whatever OCIServerAttach takes, an alias or a full
(DESCRIPTION=...), so the same binary points at oraproxy in front of a real
oracle server or at a local sqlr-listener speaking the oracle protocol, and
the two captures can be diffed.

The four attempts run in the order --attempts= names them, and the default
is all four, as in oci8.cpp: wrongpassword (the real user with a bad
password, ORA-01017), unknownuser (a user that does not exist - ORA-01017
too, since a real server answers the same way for both on purpose),
emptypassword (ORA-01005, which OCI raises client side before sending
anything, so this attempt puts nothing at all on the wire) and
correctpassword, which has to succeed on the same attach the failures ran
on. After each failed attempt the program reads OCI_ATTR_SERVER back off
the service context and reports whether it is still the handle the attach
returned - a client-side read that puts nothing on the wire, and what says
the retries ran on one connection rather than on a fresh one.

A run against a REAL server should usually ask for fewer attempts than the
default. Every wrong-password attempt counts against the account's
FAILED_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS profile limit and can lock testuser out, which would
break every other oracle test on this host; an unknown user never reaches
password verification and so cannot lock anything, which makes
--attempts=unknownuser,correctpassword the retry sequence with no lockout
risk at all. A run that does spend a wrong-password attempt should end on
correctpassword, which resets the failed count.

It exists for #10039: against sqlrelay the wrong-password and unknown-user
attempts come back ORA-03120 and ORA-24327 instead of ORA-01017, even
though the server sends ORA-01017, and no capture on file covers a modern
client retrying a login on one connection - the ones there are all
single-attempt failures.

oracleverifiernegotiation.cpp, also built by `make` here, is the
oracleprotocol suite's check of the logon negotiation (#10515). It sends
phase one of a login by hand, advertising logon types 0x05, 0x08 and 0x2a
in turn, to the 12.1, 11.2 and 10.2 listeners and to two 12.1 listeners
with an allowedlogonversion floor of 11 and of 12a, and checks the
verifier, the challenge and the trailer each client gets, or the ORA-28040
a below-floor client gets instead. Its -native run does the same in the
native encoding, and runs twice: once with the client advertising field
version 11.2 and once 12.1, since the native struct the error object and
the challenge trailer share is 8 bytes longer at 12.1 and up (#10544).

In the native encoding the ORA-28040 is checked byte for byte, size and
message included. The test builds the expected object from the field rules,
with the live pointer at offset 71 zeroed and the call number set to the
one the case sent. Those rules reproduce packet [0015] of 10544-centos6x64-
oci11-native-loginretry-unknownuser-realserver-oracle122-allowed12a.oraproxy
once the pointer is zeroed and the call number is set. Also in
the native run, at the 12.1 and 11.2 listeners, a login completes phase
one and answers the challenge with a wrong password. It checks the break
and reset and then the ORA-01017 object byte for byte. The expected
bytes come from the field rules, which reproduce packet [0017] of the
10544 oracle122 (12.1) and oracle112 (11.2) captures, so the
phase two call number, byte 21 (2 at 12.1, 0 at 11.2) and the second ORA
number at offset 131 (12.1 only) are covered. The challenge trailer is
checked at 136 bytes counting the 0x04 at field version 11.2 and 144 at
12.1 and up. The 12.1 cases are inferred: there is no 12.1 server capture,
and the 12.2 server's objects stand in for it. A 143 byte object is a real
capture only at field version 9 (10039 and the 10544 ccapcf capture), and
the module never negotiates 9.

The native struct is the 64 bit one. The module reads and writes only the
64 bit native login layout: 8 byte pointers, an 8 byte field count, and a
135 byte summary object (143 at 12.1 and up) with the pointer at offset
71. A real 32 bit server, oracle102, sends something else to a 32 bit
client natively (#10718, the 10718-fedora16x86-*-realserver-oracle102
captures): 4 byte pointers ("fe ff ff ff"), a 4 byte user size, auth mode
and field count, and a 95 byte summary object (the 93 byte OCI7 native
struct plus the 2 byte end to end field, a 4 byte live pointer at offset
63, an ORA-01017 object of 147 bytes with its message). A 64 bit client's
native login against a real server answering a representation other than
0x01 has a 4 byte field count and a 70 byte error object with no "36 01"
marker, which the 8 byte field count read does not fit either.

Two things trigger the native login, and the module triggers neither.
One is a client banner equal to the server's: the client sends a short
data type request with no type list, and the module's banner never
matches. The other is the server answering type 0x20 with a
representation other than 0x01 (a real 12.2 server answers a 64 bit
client 0x0c, and the rep20to0a capture answers a 32 bit client 0x0a):
the module always answers 0x01. A 32 bit 11.2 or 12.2 OCI client (banner
"Linuxi386/Linux-2.0.34-8.1.0", offers 0x0a first for type 0x20) is
therefore always portable against the module, at 12.1, 11.2 and 10.2,
and against a real oracle112 or oracle122 too. A 32 bit client goes
native only against a real 32 bit server with the same banner
(oracle102). If a proxy rewrites the module's banner or its answer for
type 0x20, the module reads the 4 byte pointers as 8 byte ones,
fabricates a 64 bit challenge, and the client hangs. That is a
documented limitation, not supported, and the module does not crash.

The portable run (#10717) compares the whole count prefixed error object
byte for byte as well, size and message included, for the ORA-28040
refusals of the 0x05 (O3LOGON shape) and 0x08 (modern shape) clients. It
runs at client field versions 6 and 7: the 12.1 extension (the ORA number
again and the row count before the message length) and the "00 00" at the
end of the challenge trailers are there at 7 and not at 6. The portable
run also logs in with a wrong password at the 12.1 and 11.2 listeners,
with the 11g and the 9i verifier, and checks the break and reset and then
the ORA-01017 object. The statement type is the byte that proves the fix: 2
at the 12.1 listener and 0 at the 11.2 listener (byte 11 in the modern
shape, byte 10 in the O3LOGON shape). The expected bytes reproduce the
real objects in the 10717 captures, which are 12.2 (field version 9, 6 and
1), 11.2 and 10.2 server objects. The 12.1 cases at field version 7, and
the O3LOGON shape at field version 7 and at the 11.2 listener, are
inferred. There is no capture of them.

Both runs also check the TNS flags byte of the break and reset markers
(#10720). A real server sets it to 0x20 when the negotiated TNS version is
0x013b or higher (the large header) and 0 below that, so the matrix runs
at two connect offers: 0x0140, which the module negotiates down to 0x013b,
and 0x013a, which it accepts as is. Every refusal and wrong-password login
must carry 0x20 at the first and 0 at the second. The 0x013a and lower
cases are real captures (11.2 and 10.2 servers, 0x013a and 0x0139). The
0x013b case was measured through an oraproxy that rewrote the offered
version on the way to the stock oracle122 listener (see
10720-dev-oci23-native-loginretry-unknownuser-accept013b-realserver-
oracle122.oraproxy). The module used to take the flag from the logon shape
instead of the negotiated version, so it sent 0x20 to an 11.2 client and 0
to a 10g classic login at 0x013b.

The ANO response data packet follows the same rule (#10721), and so does
every lob data packet (type 15) of a zero copy lob read. Both carry 0x20 at
0x013b and up and 0 below it, while the lob marker (TTC 0x0e) and every
other data packet are 0 at both sizes. The module used to send 0 for the
ANO response at 0x013b and 0x20 for every lob data packet. The portable and
native runs check the ANO response flags at both connect offers
(checkAnoResponseFlags() in oracleverifiernegotiation). The portable run
also checks the first lob data packet (checkLobDataFlags()): after an 11g
login below field version 12.1 the client offers zero copy, selects a clob
and reads it, and the flags must be 0x20 at the first offer and 0 at the
second. That check does not run at 12.1 (the raw client's query3 request
lacks the 12.1 fields and the select hangs) or in the native run (lobRead()
sends only the portable request). The 0x013a and 0x013c lob data cases are
real captures (OCI 23 and a genuine 11.2 client against oracle122). No real
type 15 packet exists at exactly 0x013b, so that threshold is inferred from
the markers and the ANO response measured there.

oci7.cpp, also in this directory, is oci8.cpp's counterpart written
against the legacy pre-8.0 OCI7 call interface (olog/oopen/oparse/
odefin/oexec/ofen/oexfet/oclose/ologof/odescr/obndrv) instead of the
modern OCI8 API, exercising the same set of operations for direct
wire-level comparison against oci8.cpp's captures. It only builds when
that legacy interface was detected at configure time - the headers
(ocidfn.h, ociapr.h, ocikpr.h) found under $ORACLE_HOME/rdbms/demo or
rdbms/public in a full Oracle Client install, a path Oracle Instant
Client does not populate - so it may not be present in a given build.

oci7describe.cpp, gated by the same configure-time check as oci7.cpp, is
a minimal OCI7 client for capturing TTI_DESCRIBE (0x2B) traffic: olog,
oopen, oparse, then - depending on --describe= - odefin/oexec/ofen,
then odescr, then oclose, ologof. Nothing else, so a capture of it holds
only the login, one statement, and the describe call, unlike oci7.cpp's
full test suite, which forks a second connection for its Authentication
section and makes a much noisier capture.

    ./oci7describe SID \
      [--describe=parse|exec|parseexec|fetch|outofrange|concurrent| \
                  concurrentboth|unparsedcursor|secondexec|thirdcursor| \
                  midfetch] \
                     [--defines=LIST] [USER PASSWORD [QUERY]]

The eleven variants each put a different describe on the wire: parse
(right after oparse, before any oexec), exec (after oexec, before the
first ofen), parseexec (oparse, oexec, then odescr, none of the three
bailing out if an earlier one fails - unlike exec, which bails on a
failed oparse or oexec and assumes a working select via its odefin on
columns 1-3, parseexec is the one that can reach a genuine execute-time
error, such as a too-wide insert answering ORA-12899, #9983), fetch
(after at least one ofen - the #9599 mid-fetch case), out of range
(describing a column past the end of the select list, to see what a real
server answers with), concurrent (a second cursor described while a
first cursor's fetch is still in progress, across two cursors open at
once), concurrentboth (oci7.cpp's Concurrent Cursors section call for
call - both cursors odefin'd before either is oexec'd, both executed,
fetches ping-ponged a row at a time, then closed in the reverse of the
order they opened), unparsedcursor (oci7.cpp's Server Version section -
a second cursor parsed, defined, executed and fetched while the cursor
Connect opened sits open beside it, never oparse'd), secondexec
(concurrent with only one thing changed - the cursor that is defined,
executed and fetched is the second one opened rather than the first),
thirdcursor (three cursors open at once, with the third one running the
query - the id a real server only hands out once two others are already
open), and midfetch (oci7.cpp's own "odescr - mid-fetch"
section call for call, against the four-column protocoltesttable,
including the fetches that follow the describe - the state none of the
others reach, and where a real OCI7 client segfaulted against sqlrelay,
#9810).

concurrent, concurrentboth, unparsedcursor and secondexec are four cuts
at the same question, and #9699 is why there are four. concurrent runs
clean against both a real server and sqlrelay, even though it has two
cursors open across parse, execute, fetch and describe - so two open
cursors is not by itself what fails. It differs from the two oci7.cpp
sections that do fail in three ways, and the other three variants take
one each:

    concurrentboth   both cursors odefin'd before either is oexec'd, and
                     both executed.  concurrent defines and executes the
                     first cursor only.  the defines ride inside the
                     TTI_QUERY2 an oexec sends, so a second cursor
                     carrying pending defines is a real difference in
                     what the client builds
    unparsedcursor   the idle cursor is never oparse'd.  concurrent
                     parses both.  an open, unparsed cursor sitting
                     beside an executing one is a state nothing else in
                     this tree covers
    secondexec       the cursor that runs the query is the second one
                     opened.  concurrent executes only the first cursor
                     it opens, so no capture on file shows a query
                     running on any cursor but the lowest id the server
                     handed out - and both oci7.cpp sections that fail
                     execute a cursor that is not the lowest

--defines= applies to midfetch alone and names the column positions to
odefin, as single digits: "1" (the default, and what oci7.cpp does), "3",
"12", "1234". It exists because a real server sends back only the columns
the client defined, and every capture taken before #9810 defined every
column, so nothing on file said whether the server keys that off the
positions the client named or merely off how many it named. Running
--defines=3 and --defines=1 against the same query answers that in one
run each: --defines=3 comes back with column 3's value, in slot 3.

oci7bind.cpp, gated by the same configure-time check as oci7.cpp, is a
minimal OCI7 client for capturing bind-variable traffic: olog, oopen,
oparse, obndrv, then - depending on --bind= - odefin/oexec/ofen or just
oexec, then oclose, ologof. Nothing else.

    ./oci7bind SID [--bind=VARIANT] [USER PASSWORD]

It exists for #9700. oci7.cpp's own Binds section exercises the same calls,
but it is the tail of a suite that logs in twice, creates tables and runs a
hundred other statements first, so a capture of it is far too noisy to read
a byte layout out of - the same reason oci7describe.cpp exists next to it
for describes.

The nine variants each have to be captured on their own, and each isolates
one unknown:

    selectint    one IN bind (SQLT_INT) plus one odefin, on a select - the
                  smallest packet that has both a bind block and a define
                  block in it, so it shows where one ends and the other
                  begins
    selectstr    the same with the bind SQLT_STR, so diffing the two
                  isolates the datatype and length fields
    selecttwo    two binds and two defines, which gives the stride from one
                  bind descriptor to the next
    insert       three binds and no defines at all (dml), isolating the
                  bind block with nothing behind it
    nullbind     the same insert with two of the three indicators -1
    many         bind once, oexec three times - obndrv binds by reference,
                  so this says whether the client re-marshals the binds on
                  every execute or sends them only with the first
    out          a pl/sql block with one OUT bind
    inout        a pl/sql block whose bind is read and written
    nullout      a pl/sql block that assigns NULL to its out bind

insert, nullbind and many create the protocoltestbind table themselves
(dropping any leftover first), so no other program has to have run against
the backend.

The nine 9700-* captures under samples/ were taken with it, and they are what
#9700's fix is written against. Before them no capture anywhere had
OPTION_BIND set - not the fourteen already checked in, and not any of the
working captures from the #9637/#9638/#9680/#9808/#9810 passes - so the bind
block's position and shape were unattributable to anything real, and
query2()'s OPTION_BIND path was a guess from a pre-8i wiki page that turned
out to be wrong.

oci7datestr.cpp, gated by the same configure-time check as oci7.cpp and
oci7bind.cpp, is a minimal OCI7 client for one column at a time: olog,
oopen, oparse, then odefin/oexec/ofen/oclose/ologof on a single select,
printing the fetched value in hex, and (for a SQLT_STR/SQLT_DAT define)
as a string too. Originally written for #9974 to capture a DATE column
fetched SQLT_STR instead of SQLT_DAT (see the 9974-* capture above);
#10273 extended it with two more flags rather than writing a new
program.

    ./oci7datestr SID [--query=QUERY] [--dat] [--bin] [--bindstr]
                       [--bufsize=N] [--alter=NLSDATEFORMAT]
                       [USER PASSWORD]

--query= is what to select - one column, default "select sysdate from
dual". --dat odefin's the column SQLT_DAT (the binary date form)
instead of the default SQLT_STR; --bufsize= sets the SQLT_STR/SQLT_BIN
buffer's size (default 64 - the client puts one less than this in the
define descriptor); --alter= runs an "alter session set
NLS_DATE_FORMAT='...'" before the select, to show whether the answer
moves with the session.

--bin and --bindstr were added for #10273. --bin odefin's the column
SQLT_BIN instead of SQLT_STR, so the raw wire bytes come back with no
client-side charset reinterpretation - the control a SQLT_STR capture
is diffed against (--dat wins if both --dat and --bin are given;
SQLT_BIN output skips the string line, since the bytes are not null
terminated, but still prints the hex bytes line). --bindstr skips
--query entirely and instead obndrv's the literal byte sequence
61 c3 a9 e2 82 ac (ascii 'a' plus the UTF-8 bytes of U+00E9 and U+20AC,
built byte by byte so the source file never carries a multibyte
character) as a SQLT_STR IN bind on "select :v from dual", then
odefin/oexec/ofen's it straight back on a cursor of its own - the
client-to-server half of a charset capture, kept separate from the
--query fetch path so the two never tangle.

To reproduce the fetch side of #10273's capture, pass a --query that
builds the test value from ASCII with chr(), so the client never has
to send a multibyte character in the statement text itself - for
example --query="select 'a'||chr(233)||chr(8364) from dual". chr()'s
argument is a code value in the database's own NLS_CHARACTERSET: for a
single-byte charset (e.g. WE8ISO8859P1) that lines up with the Unicode
code point for these values, but for a multibyte one (AL32UTF8) it's
the raw encoded byte value instead, so the equivalent query against an
AL32UTF8 database is select 'a'||chr(50089)||chr(14844588) from dual.
This distinction tripped up the first attempt at #10273's query (see
comment 7 on the ticket, and the strfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r1
capture entry below).

Like oci7describe.cpp and oci7bind.cpp it only builds when the
configure-time OCI7 probe finds a full ORACLE_HOME/rdbms/demo layout,
which an Instant Client install does not provide - unlike them, it has
never been wired into this directory's Makefile even on hosts that do
have that layout, since HAVE_OCI7 has never actually come up set on
any of them; it carries a hand-compile g++ command in its own header
comment instead, which is what every #10273 capture, against both
redhat9x86's real Oracle9i 9.2.0.4.0 client and dev's Instant Client
23, used to build it.

oci7transaction.cpp, gated the same way, is a minimal OCI7 client for
capturing the commit, rollback and autocommit-on/off calls (ocom, orol,
ocon, ocof) - none of which oci7.cpp's own Transactions section drove
directly before #10293, since acsite.m4's FW_CHECK_OCI7 probe didn't
check for the four symbols until that ticket extended it from twelve
checked symbols to sixteen.

    ./oci7transaction SID [--sequence=NAME] [--table=TABLE]
                           [--ddltable=TABLE] [USER PASSWORD]

--sequence= selects one variant per run: select-commit (a login, a
select and fetch, then ocom - the #9656 capture's own shape,
reproduced deliberately so its capture can be diffed against a fresh
one), dml-commit and dml-rollback (an insert into --table, default
protocoltest10293txn, then ocom or orol), empty-rollback (orol with
nothing pending), autocommit-toggle (ocon then ocof, no DML between),
autocommit-on-dml (ocon, then an insert with no explicit ocom, to
capture what a real server sends when autocommit alone drives the
commit), and autocommit-off-dml (ocof, an insert, then an explicit
ocom). Each variant runs inside its own oopen/oclose pair, then one
more oopen/oclose, so a capture shows whether the call status a commit
or rollback answers with carries into the replies that follow it in
the same session - see the 10293-*-realserver captures below, and
#10293's "Decode everything and decide" comment for what that carryover
turned out to be.

#10295 added fifteen more variants, to pin down how that call status
moves through a session: dml-dml-commit, dml-select-commit,
dml-openclose-commit, rollback-dml-commit, commit-select,
rollback-autocommit-toggle, dml-autocommit-on, autocommit-on-dml-dml,
ddl-alone and dml-ddl (which drop and create --ddltable, default
protocoltest10295ddl), plsql-null, plsql-dml-commit, dml-sqlcommit
("commit" run as a statement), dml-error-rollback and
select-for-update-commit. The program's own header comment describes
each one. See the 10295-*-realserver captures below.

#10315 added eleven more, for the cases #10295 left alone: merge-commit,
rollback-merge-commit, autocommit-on-merge, dml-ddl-misc (an insert
before each of grant, revoke, comment, rename, analyze, audit, noaudit,
drop, flashback, purge and a failing grant), select-for-update-idle,
commit-select-for-update, autocommit-on-select-for-update,
commit-describe, commit-exfet, plsql-commit-inside and dml-plsql-null.
It also added --lngflg=, oparse()'s language flag (default 2). See the
10315-*-realserver captures below.

oci8.cpp's rowid section only calls OCIRowidToChar when the configure-time
FW_CHECK_OCIROWIDTOCHAR probe found it compiles and links cleanly - some
early OCI8-era client installs export the symbol under a short
platform-specific alias with no prototype for either name anywhere in
their headers, even though the rest of the interface oci8.cpp uses is
present, so an Oracle 9.0.1 client can otherwise fail to even compile
this test program. Without it, the rowid section just confirms the
descriptor came back non-null instead of checking its base-64 external
form.

ocibannerrewritetest drives ociselect through oraproxy's banner-rewriting
mode against the real oracle122 server, and decodes the result.

    ./ocibannerrewritetest [both|s|c|native] [LISTENPORT]

This is how #9174 got the capture both #9174 and #9173 were stuck
without: a real Oracle server answering an OCI client in the portable
encoding. An OCI client compares the server's platform
banner against its own, and #10718 measured what that does. When they are
equal, the client sends a short data type request with no type list, and
both ends marshal in the client's platform encoding (native). When they
differ, the client sends the full type list, and the encoding follows the
server's answer for type 0x20: 0x01 is portable, and anything else is the
client's own platform representation, which is native. The real 12.2
server answered a 64 bit OCI 23 client 0x0c, so that pair is native
whatever the banner is, and a real server answers a 32 bit client 0x01,
so a 32 bit client is portable against a real 64 bit server whatever the
banner is. The module's banner is "SQLRelay/PortableTTC", which differs
from every client's, and the module always answers 0x01, so no client
reaches the native encoding against it. The real 12.2 server's banner
("x86_64/Linux 2.4.xx") is 19 bytes, so a proxy can swap it for any other
19 byte string inside the TTIPRO exchange without touching a length field.
This script uses "Solaris64/SunOS 5.9", which is only a stand-in of that
length.

The swap has to be done in BOTH directions. Each end runs the same
comparison, so rewriting only the server's banner puts the client in the
portable encoding and leaves the real server in the native one; the
server then cannot parse the client's portable datatype negotiation and
drops the connection with ORA-28547. With "both", both ends go portable
and a full login and select run through.

The portable encoding is not the whole story, though. Portable or not,
an OCI client against a real server marshals every ub4 as a fixed width
little endian 4 bytes and every pointer as 8 bytes, where the module and
the thin drivers use the count prefixed ("universal") form. That is
picked in the datatype negotiation: OCI offers two representations for
the integer types (25 through 33) - its platform's, then the universal
one - and a real server takes the first, while the module offers nothing
but the universal one and OCI complies. So a capture of a real server
answering OCI is in a different integer representation than anything the
module ever sends, and is not a byte-for-byte reference for it.

    ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS=1 ./ocibannerrewritetest both

fixes that. It has oraproxy rewrite the client's first representation for
those types to the universal one on the way past, so the real server has
nothing else to pick and answers OCI in exactly the representation the
module uses. The rewrite is same-length and the session runs to
completion (ociselect prints "row 1: 1"). This is what produced the
reference the #9174 fix was written against.

The rewrite is limited to the client's data type negotiation message:
the client DATA packets from the one with TTC code 0x02 until the
server's 0x02 answer, which can be more than one packet. oraproxy holds
that message's packets only until each is whole, rewrites the entries in
it, and logs the types it rewrote. It leaves an entry alone when its type
is 0, and when its second field is already 1, as with the type 0xb5 entry
OCI 23.26 sends a 10.2 server in the second packet of the message. So a
10.2 run logs 9 rewrites (types 0x19 through 0x21), not 10. Anywhere
else the same byte pattern is a run of zeros: ojdbc's TTI_QUERY3
requests, piggybacked calls and lob operations are full of them, and
before #10697 the rewrite flipped one to 1 there, which silently changed
what an ojdbc request meant and made a real 12.2 server answer one OCI
native run with ORA-03137. With the rewrite limited to the message, it is
safe to use with ojdbc and OCI native (both pass through unchanged), and
a capture taken with ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS=1 differs from one without
only in those entries. Captures below noted as "taken without
ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS" were taken before the fix, and that note is
true history.

