    9989-dev-oci23-native-biglob-realserver.oraproxy
                                     ociselect with OCISELECT_LOB=1 (login,
                                      a small clob/blob/bfile row, then a
                                      32000 byte clob - "a lob too big for
                                      one tns packet", ociselect.cpp's own
                                      words for it) against the real 10.2
                                      server at 10.0.2.3 through oraproxy,
                                      forced to reveal how a real server
                                      splits a response across TNS packets
                                      once it exceeds the negotiated sdu.
                                      the accept packet negotiates sdu 2048;
                                      the 32000 byte clob comes back as
                                      sixteen consecutive ordinary DATA
                                      packets (type 6, flags 0x00) of 2003
                                      body bytes each, then one 303 byte
                                      packet carrying the tail of the value
                                      plus the next TTC element with no gap.
                                      every continuation packet's 2 byte
                                      "data flags" field is 0x0000, same as
                                      an ordinary packet - nothing marks a
                                      packet as a continuation.  the split
                                      point falls wherever the byte count
                                      lands: the clob's own long form clr
                                      chunk markers (0xff length bytes every
                                      256 bytes) drift across TNS packet
                                      boundaries with no realignment, so a
                                      chunk boundary and a packet boundary
                                      can and do land at different offsets
                                      in different packets (#9989)
