                                      bind of a DATE (SQLT_DAT, the oracle
                                      internal date 01-JAN-2001 01:01:01,
                                      bytes 78 65 01 01 02 02 02) against
                                      "select :d from dual" - against the
                                      real 10.2 server at 10.0.2.3 through
                                      oraproxy, settling what an OCI7 client
                                      actually puts on the wire for a date
                                      bind, which no capture on file had
                                      before this. the bind descriptor's
                                      datatype byte is 12 (ORACLE_TYPE_DATE),
                                      at the same structural position a
                                      NUMBER bind's datatype byte 2 occupies
                                      in the selectint capture taken for
                                      comparison in the same session (not
                                      itself checked in - the two packets
                                      are otherwise byte-identical up to
                                      that divergence). the value is the
                                      plain short form getQuery2BindValues()
                                      already handles - length byte 7,
                                      then the seven raw date bytes, echoed
                                      back unchanged in the row that comes
                                      back. this session negotiated the
                                      NATIVE encoding with the real server
                                      (unlike sqlrelay, which only ever
                                      negotiates portable - see
                                      getQuery2Descriptors()), so the full
                                      descriptor block's byte OFFSETS here
                                      are not directly comparable to a
                                      capture against sqlrelay itself; only
                                      the datatype byte and the value's
                                      encoding, both of which read the same
                                      regardless of native vs portable, are
                                      relied on (#9986)
    9984-dev-oci23api7-native-out-realserver.oraproxy
                                     oci7bind's out variant - one OUT-only
                                      pl/sql bind, "begin :v := 7; end;"
                                      with an SQLT_INT buffer - against the
                                      real 10.2 server at 10.0.2.3 through
                                      oraproxy. settles what a real server
                                      sends back for the out bind that
                                      #9700's request-side captures proved
                                      carries nothing on the way in. the
                                      whole answer is one packet: a
                                      TTC_IO_VECTOR (0x0b) object, then a
                                      TTC_ROW_DATA (0x07) block holding the
                                      value and its indicator, then the
