                                      rewrite in both directions, so this was
                                      taken through two chained oraproxies -
                                      the client-facing one rewriting the
                                      client's banner in the "c" direction,
                                      the server-facing one rewriting the
                                      server's in the "s" direction, the
                                      capture taken at the client-facing one.
                                      Rewriting only the server side, which
                                      gets ORA-28547 against the 12.2 server,
                                      logs in fine here: this client's banner
                                      never matched the 10.2 server's to begin
                                      with, so neither end was in the native
                                      encoding for a banner rewrite to take it
                                      out of (#10048)
    10048-dev-oci23api7-bannerrewrite-universalreps-realserver.oraproxy
                                     the same again with
                                      ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS=1, so the real
                                      server answers in the count prefixed
                                      integer representation the module uses.
                                      This is the reference capture: what a
                                      real 10.2 server sends THIS client in
                                      the module's own encoding, and it is not
                                      what the module sends. [0014] is
                                      "08 | 01 40 | 40 | 64 hex chars" then a
                                      31 byte summary, where the module sends
                                      "08 | 01 20 | 20 | 32 hex chars" then a
                                      29 byte summary. The summary difference
                                      is exactly two bytes, "01 02" - a length
                                      prefixed field carrying 2 - inserted
                                      right after the end of call status;
                                      every field after it is identical,
                                      including the call number. [0015] is the
                                      client's TTI_LOGON_PRESENT_PWD, portable
                                      framing, with a 64 character password
                                      field, and the session runs to a clean
                                      logoff (#10048)
    10048-dev-oci23api7-universalreps-realserver.oraproxy
                                     the control for the pair above:
                                      ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS=1 with no banner
                                      rewrite at all. Identical in every field
                                      to the capture above - same framing,
                                      same 64 character key, same 31 byte
                                      summary - which is what pins the banner
                                      as contributing nothing and the data
                                      type negotiation's integer
                                      representation as the thing that picks
                                      the challenge's framing (#10048)
    10048-dev-oci23api7-ccap05-realserver.oraproxy
                                     the same run with one byte of the
                                      client's data type negotiation rewritten
                                      on the way past: compile capability
                                      index 4, CCAP_LOGON_TYPES, from this
                                      client's 0xef to the 0x05 a genuine
                                      OCI7 client advertises. Exactly one byte
                                      of packet [0009]'s 1967 differs from the
                                      unrewritten capture, at offset 21, and
                                      the real server's answer at [0014] drops
                                      from a 64 character session key to a 32
                                      character one. Same server, same
                                      account, same everything else. The
                                      summary stays the 66 byte one, so the
                                      key size and the summary shape are two
                                      separate client-driven selectors. The
                                      client then fails locally with ORA-12642
                                      rather than hanging - it advertised 0x05
                                      but is still Instant Client 23
                                      underneath. Taken with a scratch proxy
                                      rather than oraproxy, whose rewrite
                                      takes strings, not raw bytes (#10048)
    10048-dev-oci23api7-portable-login-sqlrelay-o5logon-success.oraproxy
                                     the confirmation experiment's real
                                      session against the throwaway o5logon
                                      build, oci7bind --bind=selectint as
                                      testuser, logging into oracleprotocoloci7
                                      for real rather than through a rewriting
                                      proxy: login, open, parse, bind, define,
                                      execute, fetch, close and logoff, 35
                                      packets end to end. The first capture on
                                      file of this client actually logging into
                                      sqlrelay, as against the synthetic
                                      key64-unblocked capture above. The
                                      O3LOGON challenge and login answer are
                                      byte-shape-identical to the
                                      bannerrewrite-universalreps-
                                      realserver.oraproxy reference: a 64
                                      character session key at portable length-
                                      prefixed width, then the 31 byte summary,
                                      then a login answer of the same shape
                                      (#10048)
    10048-dev-oci23api7-portable-describe-realserver.oraproxy
                                     the same client's odescr() describe
                                      exchange against the real 10.2 server,
                                      taken with ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS=1 so
                                      the server answers in the module's own
                                      count-prefixed representation. The
                                      describe response is 95 bytes, ending 09
                                      01 01 01 09 - three length-prefixed
                                      fields more than the module sends: one
                                      before the 60 80 pair, one ahead of the
                                      command type, and one ahead of the column
                                      name blob. Reference capture for the
                                      describe-response gap filed as #10051
                                      (#10048)
    10048-dev-oci23api7-portable-describe-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     the matching half against
                                      oracleprotocoloci7 with the confirmation
                                      experiment's login fix in place: the same
                                      client's describe response is 89 bytes,
                                      ending 09 01 01 00, three fields short of
                                      the real server's shape above. The
                                      module's describe response was decoded
                                      from a genuine OCI7 client and is correct
                                      for that client; this client wants the
                                      wider shape - a separate defect from the
                                      login fix, newly reachable now that login
                                      succeeds, filed as #10051 (#10048)
    10053-dev-oci23api7-portable-oexecmidfetch-realserver.oraproxy
                                     this client's oparse/oexec of "select
                                      1/(level-2) from dual connect by
                                      level<=3" (row 2 divides by zero)
                                      against the real 10.2 server, taken
                                      with oci7describe --describe=parseexec
                                      rather than the noisy full oci7
                                      suite. Portable encoding: the client's
                                      banner ("x86_64/Linux 2.4.xx", packet
                                      [0007]) doesn't match the server's
                                      ("Linuxi386/Linux-2.0.34-8.1.0",
                                      packet [0008]). oci7describe itself -
                                      genuine Oracle-vendor OCI decoding the
                                      wire response, not a hand parse -
                                      reports "oexec ok" for the
                                      TTI_EXECUTE request in packet [0029],
                                      answered by packet [0030]: the server
                                      never evaluates row 2 until a fetch
                                      actually asks for it, since
                                      TTI_EXECUTE carries no inline prefetch
                                      the way TTI_QUERY3 does. Confirms the
                                      module's existing oexec() behavior for
                                      this client class was already correct;
                                      the bug this capture settled was in
                                      oci7.cpp's own test expectation, fixed
                                      under #10053
    10054-dev-oci23api7-portable-binds-datatypes-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     the whole oci7 suite, run as
                                      "./oci7 sqlrelayoci7 --only=connect"
                                      against the oracleprotocoloci7
                                      instance with #10051's describe fix in
                                      place - the #10054 baseline. 1361
                                      packets, noisy on purpose: this is the
                                      only capture on file of the full suite
                                      against sqlrelay, and it holds both of
                                      the failure clusters #10054 is about.
                                      --only=connect keeps the run to one
                                      TCP connection, so oraproxy captures
                                      all of it without --skip.
                                      Binds, "oexec - bind once, execute
                                      many": the first execute rides
                                      TTI_QUERY2 ([0293], answered ok by
                                      [0294]), and the second and third ride
                                      a bare TTI_EXECUTE that re-marshals
                                      the bind values ([0295] and [0297],
                                      the three values "c1 2a"/"manychar"/
                                      "manyvarchar" visible in the
                                      payload). The module answers both with
                                      ORA-01007, "variable not in select
                                      list" ([0296] and [0298]), so only one
                                      of the three rows lands.
                                      Datatypes, "odescr - every type": the
                                      module's TTI_DESCRIBE response for
                                      "select * from protocoltesttypes"
                                      ([0443] asks, [0444] answers, 375 body
                                      bytes, 11 columns) is not accepted by
                                      this client. It sends a break
                                      (PACKET_MARKER, [0445]) instead of
                                      reading on, the module answers
                                      ORA-01013 "user requested cancel of
                                      current operation" ([0447]), and the
                                      client reports ORA-03146 "invalid
                                      buffer length passed to a system call"
                                      to the caller. ORA-03146 appears
                                      nowhere in the capture - it is raised
                                      client side, not sent. The same
                                      describe/break/ORA-01013 cycle repeats
                                      12 times, once per assertion in that
                                      test, and the section recovers at
                                      "odefin - every type" (#10054)

The following captures document #10273 (a real 9i/OCI7 client
misreading multibyte SQLT_STR text, traced to the forced 9i charset
default of 31/WE8ISO8859P1 disagreeing with an AL32UTF8 backend). They
group into three sets: real-server references (what a genuine 9i client
does against an honest server, no SQL Relay involved), SQL Relay
captures taken before any fix (reproducing the bug), and captures from
a throwaway, uncommitted experimental build (evidence the fix shape
works, not the shipped fix - see #10273 comment 9 for the build's
revisions v1-v4). Every run used oci7datestr.cpp's --bin/--bindstr/
--query= flags (documented above) from the real Oracle9i 9.2.0.4.0
client on redhat9x86, through oraproxy on dev.firstworks.com, unless
noted otherwise. "R1" etc. below are the run labels #10273 comments 7-9
use; .out holds the program's stdout with the NLS_LANG in effect on the
first line, .debug (where present) is a debug="protocols" sqlrelay log
slice for that one session.

Real-server references (#10273 comment 7) - a genuine 9i/OCI7 client
against oracle122, which honestly declares charset 873 (AL32UTF8):

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r1.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r1.out
                                     R1: select 'a'||chr(50089)||chr(14844588)
                                      from dual, odefin SQLT_STR,
                                      NLS_LANG=AMERICAN_AMERICA.AL32UTF8.
                                      Decisive: the client prints the correct
                                      bytes 61 c3 a9 e2 82 ac. Proves a real
                                      OCI7 SQLT_STR client reads multibyte text
                                      correctly when the server honestly
                                      declares its real charset (873) - the
                                      ticket's original client-side framing is
                                      wrong. TTIPRO charset 873, TTC 02 flags
                                      0x01, login/parse strings raw and 1x
                                      sized, row bytes 07 06 61 c3 a9 e2 82 ac.

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-binfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r2.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-binfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r2.out
                                     R2: select utl_raw.cast_to_raw(...) from
                                      dual, odefin SQLT_BIN - the raw-bytes
                                      control. Same row bytes as R1 (07 06 61
                                      c3 a9 e2 82 ac), confirming R1's bytes
                                      are the true wire bytes, not an artifact
                                      of the SQLT_STR define.

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-binfetch-varchar2-ora01465-al32utf8-realserver-r2a.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-binfetch-varchar2-ora01465-al32utf8-realserver-r2a.out
                                     R2a: odefin SQLT_BIN directly on the plain
                                      VARCHAR2 query (not cast_to_raw). A real
                                      server answers ORA-01465 'invalid hex
                                      number' - it parses VARCHAR2-as-SQLT_BIN
                                      text as hex, so this is not a raw-bytes
                                      control for a text column. Flags a
                                      pre-existing, out-of-scope divergence:
                                      SQL Relay answers this with raw bytes
                                      instead (see the sqlrelay-before-r2a
                                      entry below).

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strfetch-clob-al32utf8-realserver-r3.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strfetch-clob-al32utf8-realserver-r3.out
                                     R3: select
                                      to_clob('a'||chr(50089)||chr(14844588))
                                      from dual, odefin SQLT_STR. Clean: bytes
                                      61 c3 a9 e2 82 ac. The row uses the 0xfe
                                      long-form clr carrying raw UTF-8, not
                                      UTF-16 - same define descriptor as R1
                                      (charset 873).

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strfetch-we8iso8859p1-realserver-r4.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strfetch-we8iso8859p1-realserver-r4.out
                                     R4: same as R1 but
                                      NLS_LANG=AMERICAN_AMERICA.WE8ISO8859P1 on
                                      the client. Decisive: the server sends
                                      the same row as R1, and the client
                                      legitimately converts it to bytes 61 e9
                                      bf (the euro sign, having no 8859-1 code
                                      point, becomes Oracle's bf replacement
                                      byte) - correct, honest client behavior,
                                      not corruption. This is also the run that
                                      reproduces #9664's length inflation for
                                      real against a server that truthfully
                                      declares 873: TTC 02 flags become 0x02,
                                      login/parse strings gain a length prefix,
                                      and every declared size triples (user
                                      0x18=3x8, host 0x4b=3x25, ALTER SESSION
                                      0x2e2=3x246, osql7 0x8d=3x47, define
                                      buffer 0xbd=3x63).

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strbind-al32utf8-realserver-r5.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strbind-al32utf8-realserver-r5.out
                                     R5: --bindstr - obndrv SQLT_STR binds the
                                      literal bytes 61 c3 a9 e2 82 ac on select
                                      :v from dual, then fetches it back,
                                      NLS_LANG=AL32UTF8. Clean round trip, no
                                      client-side conversion: bind descriptor
                                      charset 873, value goes out as the six
                                      raw UTF-8 bytes.

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strbind-we8iso8859p1-realserver-r5b.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-strbind-we8iso8859p1-realserver-r5b.out
                                     R5b: R5 again with NLS_LANG=WE8ISO8859P1.
                                      Decisive for the bind direction: the
                                      client itself converts the six UTF-8
                                      bytes to AL32UTF8 before sending, using
                                      its own WE8ISO8859P1-to-AL32UTF8 table,
                                      producing 07 0c 61 c3 83 c2 a9 c3 a2 ef
                                      bf bd c2 ac - byte for byte the
                                      corruption pattern in the ticket's
                                      description, including ef bf bd (U+FFFD)
                                      for the 0x82 byte. Confirms the U+FFFD
                                      substitution is a genuine 9i client table
                                      quirk for the 0x80-0x9f range, reproduced
                                      here with no SQL Relay involved. Bind max
                                      size is declared 0x12=3x6.

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-afcfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r7.oraproxy
                                     SQLT_AFC (fixed CHAR) padding reference:
                                      select cast('ab' as char(5)), AL32UTF8.
                                      The server pads to the declared buffer
                                      size (64 bytes) - confirms putField()'s
                                      existing SQLT_AFC padding needs no change
                                      for this ticket.

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-afcfetch-we8iso8859p1-realserver-r7b.oraproxy
                                     The same SQLT_AFC run with
                                      NLS_LANG=WE8ISO8859P1: the server pads to
                                      192 bytes, 3x the AL32UTF8 run's 64 - the
                                      same declared-size inflation R4 shows,
                                      now confirmed for a fixed-width column
                                      too.

    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-describe-al32utf8-realserver-r6.oraproxy
    10273-redhat9x86-oci7-describe-al32utf8-realserver-r6.out
                                     R6: oci7describe --describe=parse against
                                      the R1 query, AL32UTF8. Decisive for the
                                      describe path: the real server sends the
                                      TTI_DESCRIBE names blob as '0b 00' plus
                                      raw NUM"TXT"DT text with NO length
                                      prefix, unlike every other 9i describe
                                      capture on file (which are all against
                                      charset 31). Charset field on each column
                                      is 69 03 (873), matching the define
                                      descriptor charset seen in R1.

    10273-redhat9x86-sqlplus-query-we8iso8859p1-realserver-r8.oraproxy
                                     R8: sqlplus (not oci7datestr),
                                      NLS_LANG=WE8ISO8859P1, against oracle122
                                      (873). A second, independent client
                                      program confirming the 3x size inflation
                                      live (ALTER SESSION 141 bytes for 47,
                                      define buffer 189 for 63) and showing the
                                      TTC 02 flags byte a real client sends is
                                      program-dependent, not just
                                      charset-dependent: sqlplus sends 0x02
                                      here where oci7datestr sends 0x01 under
                                      the matching AL32UTF8 case - the module
                                      must key off the bit the client actually
                                      sends, never predict it from charsets.

    10273-redhat9x86-sqlplus-query-we8iso8859p1-realserver-oracle102-r8b.oraproxy
                                     The same sqlplus run against oracle102
                                      (declares 31, WE8ISO8859P1 client -
                                      charsets match, no conversion). Flags
                                      byte 0x12 - confirms 0x02
                                      (ENCODING_CONV_LENGTH) is clear only when
                                      client and declared charset genuinely
                                      match, independent of which program is
                                      driving.

    10273-dev-oci23api7-strfetch-al32utf8-realserver-r1c.oraproxy
                                     R1 repeated from dev.firstworks.com using
                                      Instant Client 23's legacy OCI7 call
                                      support (the ticket's second named client
                                      class), against oracle122 directly. Same
                                      shape as the redhat9x86 R1: flags 0x01,
                                      raw unprefixed login/osql7 text, 1x
                                      sizes, unprefixed 64-hex O3LOGON
                                      challenge key. Corroborates that R1's
                                      clean result is not specific to the
                                      9.2.0.4.0 client build.

SQL Relay captures taken before any fix (#10273 comment 8) - the
