                                     R1 against the shipped fix: client prints
                                      61 c3 a9 e2 82 ac, retlen=6 - matches the
                                      realserver-r1 and exp873-r1 captures
                                      above exactly.

Each of the 9808-*, 9810-*, 9976-* and 9700-*.oraproxy captures above is
oraproxy's own capture format (see "oraproxy" below), taken by interposing
oraproxy locally on the client host and pointing the client's tnsnames.ora
at it, rather than a raw tcpdump - a real NIC's MSS is too small for
oradecode's one-frame-one-TNS-chunk assumption to hold. oradecode reads them
with no conversion.

Every column in these four multicol-*.cap captures is a NUMBER column
defined SQLT_STR (the legacy putField() character-array arm) - only the
column count and fetch mode vary. They evidence the legacy fetch path's
row/column framing across column counts, not any additional Oracle
datatype; see #9704 for datatype coverage itself.

    9656-redhat9x86-oci7-native-commitfetch3-realserver.oraproxy
    9656-redhat9x86-oci7-native-shiftedfetch3-realserver.oraproxy
    9656-redhat9x86-oci7-native-shiftedexfet3-realserver.oraproxy
                                     the three captures attached to #9656
                                      comment 2 as raw base64 tcpdump text
                                      (snaplen 96, so some packets are cut
                                      off), archived here in oraproxy's own
                                      format for #10293 rather than left to
                                      be pulled out of a ticket comment.
                                      commitfetch3 is the capture #10292's
                                      sendCommitResponse() fix was built
                                      from - a real server committing after
                                      a fetch, not any DML, packets [0028],
                                      [0040] and [0052] each answering
                                      TTI_COMMIT with the 7 raw bytes
                                      00 00 09 05 00 00 00. shiftedfetch3
                                      and shiftedexfet3 are the same
                                      session's other two capture files,
                                      kept alongside it rather than left
                                      behind in the same comment.
                                      test/protocol/oracle/pcap2oracap
                                      converted all three from tcpdump to
                                      oraproxy's format, zero-padding and
                                      warning on stderr wherever the
                                      snaplen cut a packet's real payload
                                      short - no capture here needed that,
                                      but the warning path exists for a
                                      future one that does (#10293).

pcap2oracap, in this directory, is the converter #9656's captures needed:
a small Python script that reads a raw tcpdump/libpcap file, follows its
Ethernet/IPv4/TCP framing, and writes each direction's payload out as an
oraproxy-format capture oradecode can read directly, the same way the
9808-*, 9810-*, 9976-* and 9700-*.oraproxy captures above already are.
It reconstructs each segment's real length from the IP header, and where
a capture's snaplen cut a segment's payload short, pads it with zeros and
prints which packet and direction it padded, rather than guessing at the
missing bytes silently.

    ./pcap2oracap [--server-port=N] PCAPFILE CAPTUREFILE

