    10700-dev-ojdbc-failedstmt-realserver-oracle122.oraproxy
                                     ojdbc 23.26 (a scratch program: each
                                      failure shape seven times on one
                                      connection, then a good select, with
                                      the session's opened cursors current
                                      read from v$sesstat between shapes
                                      over a separate sysdba session)
                                      against the real 12.2 server, through
                                      oraproxy without
                                      ORAPROXY_UNIVERSAL_REPS.  [0015] reads
                                      the session's sid.  [0017] executes
                                      "select * from t10700_nonexistent"
                                      with cursor id 0.  The server answers
                                      with a break and a reset marker
                                      ([0018] and [0019]), takes the
                                      client's reset ([0020]), and sends the
                                      error summary at [0021]: 04 01 01 01
                                      06 00 02 03 ae 00 00 01 01 01 0e 03,
                                      which names cursor 1 (01 01) and error
                                      position 14 (01 0e).  The server keeps
                                      the failed statement's cursor open.
                                      ojdbc closes it only when the
                                      Statement is closed, with a
                                      close-cursors piggyback in front of
                                      its next call (69 06 01 01 01 01 01 at
                                      [0022]).  Statements left open ([0054]
                                      to [0088]) get cursors 1 and 3 to 8,
                                      no close follows, and opened cursors
                                      current goes from 2 to 9.  Closing
                                      them at the end sends one piggyback
                                      naming all of them ([0493]), and the
                                      count drops from 12 to 4.  A reused
                                      Statement ([0091] on) sends its failed
                                      cursor 9 back in each re-execute, and
                                      the answers name 9 again.  ojdbc
                                      closes the prepared, ORA-00900, dml,
                                      batch and fetch error shapes the same
                                      way after each failure.  A dml error
                                      ([0215]) is return parameters and then
                                      the summary.  A fetch that fails
                                      partway ([0336]) is followed by a
                                      cancel-cursors and a close-cursors
                                      piggyback for the same cursor
                                      ([0337]).  The count's rise from 10 to
                                      12 during the dml shapes is recursive
                                      cursors (#10700)

    10700-dev-ojdbc-failedstmt-open302-realserver-oracle122.oraproxy
                                     the same program, with 302 failures on
                                      Statements left open, against the same
                                      server, whose open_cursors is 300.
                                      The answers name cursors 1 and 3 to
                                      300 ([0019] to [1509]).  The 300th
                                      failure gets ORA-01000 at [1514],
                                      after the same break and reset
                                      markers, with cursor 0 in the summary,
                                      and so does the good select ([1529]).
                                      Closing the Statements sends one
                                      close-cursors piggyback naming all 299
                                      cursors ([1530]), and the select
                                      behind it works.  So a real server
                                      holds a failed statement's cursor
