The v11 time zone file for the oci8 run
---------------------------------------

test.sh runs oci8 a second time with ORA_TZFILE pointing at
test/protocol/oracle/timezlrg_11.dat (#10690). The module announces time
zone file version 11 (DB_TIMEZONE_VERSION in src/protocols/oracle.cpp). A
client whose file is also version 11 gets timestamps with time zones in
the UTC form, and one with any other version gets the local form. OCI
decodes the rows by the form it expects, so a module that sent the wrong
form would give oci8 wrong times and offsets, and the existing checks on
those values catch it. Without the file, oci8 uses its own file (version
45 for OCI 23.26) and only the local form is tested.

The file is not checked in (.gitignore lists /timezlrg_*.dat). The test
instance skips the run with a message when it is missing. To get it, copy
it from the oracle122 server's zoneinfo directory:

    /u01/app/oracle/product/12.2.0/oracore/zoneinfo/timezlrg_11.dat

It is 787272 bytes, md5 352af9ddeb92f35ffab00ff6aef06bd7. For example,
from a host that can ssh to oracle122:

    ssh 10.0.2.1 "cat /u01/app/oracle/product/12.2.0/oracore/zoneinfo/timezlrg_11.dat" \
                                > test/protocol/oracle/timezlrg_11.dat

timezlrg_26.dat is in the same directory. Any other version works as a
client file too: the module only compares the client's version with 11.

Running oci8 with the genuine Oracle 9.2.0.4 client (redhat9x86)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

oci8 builds and runs against the 9.2.0.4 client on redhat9x86, against
real oracle122 ("native") and against a listener. Two things to know
(#10711):

* NLS_LANG must be set. With none, the 9i client defaults to US7ASCII,
  and against a database whose character set is AL32UTF8 (oracle122) it
  dies right after OCISessionBegin with "Internal heap ERROR 17112",
  OCI-21500 and a segfault. sqlplus does the same, so it is the client
  and not the test or the module. NLS_LANG=AMERICAN_AMERICA.WE8ISO8859P1
  runs oci8 to the end; with ...AL32UTF8 the login works but every lob
  call that converts text fails with ORA-24812 and a later call crashes.
* The 9.2 headers have no OCIRowidToChar, so build without
  HAVE_OCIROWIDTOCHAR (configure never ran there, so a config.h copy
  without that line first on the include path does it):

    g++ -g -o oci8 -Iinc -I/usr/local/firstworks/include \
        -I$ORACLE_HOME/rdbms/public -I$ORACLE_HOME/rdbms/demo \
        oci8.cpp -L$ORACLE_HOME/lib -lclntsh \
        -L/usr/local/firstworks/lib -lrudiments -lpthread -ldl

Run it as "./oci8 native" or "./oci8 sqlrelay12c" with TNS_ADMIN pointing
at a tnsnames.ora that names the targets and NLS_LANG set. Some checks
fail with this client against the real server too, because the 9.2
client lacks features (time zone regions, timestamps with local time
zones, the 12.2 server version string): 32 fail against oracle122, and
the same 32 fail against a 12.1 listener whose backend is oracle122, with
the same output line for line (#10716). Before it, the temporary lob
calls and an nclob read for a pre-10g client were unanswered.

The 9.2.0.4 client's timestamp binds (#10710): oci8's bind sections
(OCIBindByName of a timestamp, a timestamp with time zone and a
timestamp with local time zone, with and without an indicator, and a
named region) give the same results through the 12.1 listener as against
real oracle122, in the same run as above - none of the failing checks
there are binds. So the module reads the 9i client's bind descriptors
(the scale rule in getQuery3BindDescriptor()) correctly. No 10g OCI client
or go toolchain is on any host here, so those two clients stay
unmeasured.

The three real Oracle test servers on this network
--------------------------------------------------

Not discoverable anywhere else in this tree:

    oracle122centos7x64.firstworks.com   10.0.2.1:1521   Oracle 12.2
    oracle112centos5x64.firstworks.com   10.0.2.2:1521   Oracle 11.2
    oracle102centos4x86 (10.0.2.3)        10.0.2.3:1521   Oracle 10.2

The first two take user testuser / password testpassword against SID
ora1 (see oracle.java). As of this writing, the 11.2 server's testuser
account has an expired password (ORA-28001) - that needs a DBA to reset
it before a full login can be captured there again; it is unrelated to
oraproxy or oradecode, both of which handle the error path correctly
regardless.

The third, oracle102, is the only one of the three old enough to speak
OCI7/O3LOGON - it's the backend the oracleprotocoloci7 test instance
uses (see test/sqlrelay.conf.d/oracleprotocol.conf.in). Its hostname is
not recorded anywhere in this tree beyond this line; only its IP,
10.0.2.3, is confirmed (#9664 comment 21).

NLS_CHARACTERSET, confirmed live (nls_charset_id(), #10273): oracle102
is WE8ISO8859P1 (charset id 31); oracle122 is AL32UTF8 (charset id
873). Both also carry NCHAR AL16UTF16 (charset id 2000). This is what
oracleprotocoloci7's charset="31" (test/sqlrelay.conf.d/
oracleprotocol.conf.in) matches for oracle102, but not for oracle122 or
for oracleprotocoloci7utf8's own AL32UTF8-connected backend - see #10273.

oracle122's server-side login policy was changed for #10273, and is
still in that changed state as of this writing:
/u01/app/oracle/product/12.2.0/network/admin/sqlnet.ora on
oracle122centos7x64.firstworks.com got
SQLNET.ALLOWED_LOGON_VERSION_SERVER=8 appended (the original is backed
up alongside it as sqlnet.ora.pre-10273), and testuser's password was
reset to its own existing value (alter user testuser identified by
testpassword) to regenerate a 10G/DES password verifier alongside its
existing 11G/12C ones - the O3LOGON handshake a pre-10g OCI7 client
uses needs one. Together these let a real pre-10g OCI7 client (the
genuine Oracle9i 9.2.0.4.0 client on redhat9x86 used throughout
#10273's captures) log into oracle122 directly with its own
DESCRIPTION connect string - no oraproxy, and no separate scoped
listener, needed for that login itself. oracle122 is a pure testbed
with no sensitive data, so this was done in place rather than standing
up a separate AL32UTF8 database elsewhere.

oracle122's DMP_DIR directory object (/home/oracle) also has read and
write granted to the backend user dev (grant read, write on directory
dmp_dir to dev, #10712), so the oci8 and ojdbc runs can write a file with
utl_file and read it back through a bfile. Without the grant they skip
that part and only check the missing file behavior.

This is intended to stay in place permanently, not just for #10273's
duration - the user asked for OCI7 access to oracle122 to be kept
enabled for future OCI7 testing (#10273's finish comment). The
sqlnet.ora.pre-10273 backup is kept in place in case a future need
arises to compare against the original, unmodified file, but no
teardown step reverts this.
